<%= CsuiteFinderWeb.Layout.head(
      title: "Privacy — CSuiteFinder",
      description: "What CSuiteFinder collects, why it holds it, how long it keeps it, and how to have it removed.",
      nav: :privacy,
      css: CsuiteFinderWeb.Layout.page_css(:privacy)
    ) %>
<header>
  <h1>Privacy</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    What we collect, why we hold it, how long we keep it, and how to have it
    removed. Written plainly, because a policy nobody can read protects nobody.
  </p>
  <p class="quiet">Last updated <%= @updated_on %>.</p>
</header>

<p class="quiet">
  This service holds personal data about two different groups of people, and they
  are owed different explanations. <strong>Customers</strong> are the people who
  open an account here. <strong>Contacts</strong> are the people whose work
  details are looked up through it — who never came to this site, and in most
  cases have never heard of it. The second group is the larger one, and that
  section is written for them.
</p>

<h2 class="section-label" id="customers">If you have an account</h2>

<p class="quiet">We hold what is needed to run the account and bill it:</p>

<table class="plain">
  <tr>
    <th>Your email address</th>
    <td>
      How you sign in and how we reach you about the account. Required — it is
      the only identifier an account has.
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <th>Your name</th>
    <td>Optional. Held only if you give it.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <th>Your password</th>
    <td>
      Stored as a bcrypt hash. We cannot read it, and we cannot tell you what it
      is — only replace it.
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <th>Your API keys</th>
    <td>
      Stored as a SHA-256 hash, plus the first sixteen characters so you can tell
      one key from another in a list. The full key is shown once, when it is
      created, and is not recoverable afterwards.
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <th>Balance and credit</th>
    <td>What you have bought, what you have been granted, and when it expires.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <th>Payments</th>
    <td>
      The amount, the currency, the status, the processor's reference numbers, and
      the response the processor sent us — which typically includes the name and
      email on the payment and the brand and last four digits of the card.
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <th>Usage</th>
    <td>
      Which endpoints your key called, how many answers were billed, and when. This
      is what your invoice is built from.
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <th>Failed sign-ins</th>
    <td>
      A counter and a lockout time. Kept to make guessing a password expensive.
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<p class="quiet">
  <strong>We never see your card.</strong> Payment happens on the processor's own
  hosted page — Square, Stripe or PayPal — and card numbers are never entered on
  this site, never transmitted through it and never stored by it.
</p>

<p class="quiet">
  Our application sets <strong>no cookies of its own</strong>. Your key, your
  session token and two display preferences are kept in your browser's
  <code>localStorage</code>, which stays on your device. Clearing your browser
  data signs you out. The analytics services described below set their own
  storage.
</p>

<h2 class="section-label" id="contacts">If you are someone we found</h2>

<p class="quiet">
  You are reading this because your work contact details may be held here. You did
  not give them to us, so here is the whole picture.
</p>

<p class="quiet"><strong>What we may hold about you:</strong> your name, your work
  email address, your employer's domain and company name, your job title,
  department and seniority, your LinkedIn and X/Twitter profile URLs, a work phone
  number and what is publicly known about that number's type, carrier, country,
  region and city, and a general location. We also keep the complete response our
  data suppliers returned about you, which can contain more than the fields listed
  here.</p>

<p class="quiet"><strong>Where it came from:</strong> three places, and the third
  is the one worth reading twice.</p>

<p class="quiet">Commercial business-contact data suppliers. The email format used
  by your employer — which, once known, lets an address be constructed rather than
  looked up. And <strong>addresses our customers ask us to check</strong>: when
  someone submits an address to find out whether mail to it will arrive, and the
  mailbox confirms that it will, we keep the address and the company it belongs
  to. So an address can reach us because somebody you have never dealt with had
  it in a list and wanted to know it was live.</p>

<p class="quiet">We keep it only when the mailbox genuinely confirmed it. An
  address that bounced is not kept, and neither is one at a company whose mail
  server accepts everything, because that proves nothing. We do not collect any of
  it from you, and we do not scrape your employer's website ourselves.</p>

<p class="quiet"><strong>Why we hold it:</strong> to answer a customer's question
  of the form "what is this person's work email address, and will mail to it
  arrive?" Our lawful basis is legitimate interests — business-to-business
  contact, in a professional capacity, using details that identify you at work
  rather than at home. You have the right to object to that, and objecting is
  enough; you do not have to give a reason.</p>

<p class="quiet"><strong>What we do not do:</strong> we do not send you marketing,
  we do not sell lists, we do not build a profile of your behaviour, and we do not
  knowingly hold home addresses, personal email accounts, or any special-category
  data — health, beliefs, politics, ethnicity, sexuality, trade union membership.
  We do not hold data about children.</p>

<p class="quiet"><strong>What our customers do with it is their responsibility.</strong>
  We supply an answer to a question. The person who sends you a message is the one
  who decided to, and they are responsible under their own obligations for doing
  so lawfully.</p>

<h2 class="section-label" id="retention">How long we keep it</h2>

<p class="quiet">
  Every record carries a review date. Contact records are re-checked after ninety
  days, deliverability results after thirty, company details after sixty, and an
  employer's email format after a hundred and eighty.
</p>

<p class="quiet">
  <strong>Be clear about what that date means: it is when we look again, not when
  we delete.</strong> A record that is still correct is refreshed and kept, and if
  a re-check fails we keep the previous answer and mark it stale rather than
  discard it. In practice a record stays until it is deleted on request. We would
  rather say so than imply an expiry that does not happen.
</p>

<p class="quiet">
  Account, payment and usage records are kept for as long as the account exists
  and afterwards for as long as tax and accounting rules require.
</p>

<h2 class="section-label" id="rights">Your rights, and how to use them</h2>

<p class="quiet">
  Whether you are a customer or a contact, you can ask us to tell you what we hold
  about you, correct it, delete it, or stop processing it. If you are a contact and
  you want to be removed, saying so is enough — there is nothing to justify.
</p>

<p class="quiet">
  One address for all of it:
  <a href="mailto:<%= @privacy_contact %>"><%= @privacy_contact %></a>. Include the
  email address or name you want us to look for.
</p>

<p class="quiet">
  <strong>What this is, honestly:</strong> a person reading a mailbox and running
  the deletion by hand. There is no self-service privacy dashboard, no automated
  request system, and no team on a rota — this is a small operation and we would
  rather tell you that than imply a machine is handling it. It does mean a request
  is answered when it is read, and we are not going to put a number on that here
  that we cannot stand behind. If a legal deadline applies to you, it applies to us
  and we will meet it.
</p>

<p class="quiet">
  Removal takes the record out of our store. It cannot reach back into copies a
  customer has already downloaded, and it cannot stop the original supplier from
  holding it — so if you want to be out of this market generally, it is worth
  asking the large business-data brokers directly as well.
</p>

<p class="quiet">
  If you are in the UK, EU, or a US state with its own privacy statute, you also
  have the right to complain to your data protection authority.
</p>

<h2 class="section-label" id="analytics">Analytics on this website</h2>

<p class="quiet">
  Two measurement tools run on these marketing pages. Neither runs on your account
  page, because that page shows an API key and carries password fields.
</p>

<p class="quiet">
  <strong>They load when the page does.</strong> There is no cookie banner on this
  site and nothing here asks your permission first, so you should assume both have
  already run by the time you read this. We are telling you rather than letting you
  work it out. If you would rather they did not, a tracker blocker or Google's own
  opt-out extension stops them; we do not currently offer a switch of our own.
</p>

<p class="quiet">
  <strong>Google Analytics</strong> records which pages are viewed, how far down
  them people read, how long they stay and which links they click. Query strings
  are deliberately stripped before anything is sent, so an address carried in a
  link cannot end up in the analytics property. Google sets its own cookies and
  acts as a processor under its own terms.
</p>

<p class="quiet">
  <strong>SeriouslySimpleAnalytics</strong> records the same shape of thing —
  pageviews, engaged time, scroll depth, clicks and the path taken between pages.
  It derives an approximate country, region and city, and the browser, operating
  system and device type. <strong>It does not store your IP address.</strong> The
  address is hashed with a salt that changes daily and then discarded, which is
  enough to notice one machine spraying traffic and not enough to identify you or
  to link today's visit to tomorrow's.
</p>

<p class="quiet">
  We also send our own service a count of API calls — how many, to which endpoint,
  how many were answered from cache. Those counts never carry a customer's email,
  domain, name, account or key.
</p>

<p class="quiet">
  Passwords, email addresses, API keys and tokens are stripped from our server logs
  before they are written.
</p>

<h2 class="section-label" id="sharing">Who else sees it</h2>

<p class="quiet">
  Our payment processors, for the payment you make. Our hosting provider, which
  runs the servers. The analytics services named above. The business-data suppliers
  that answer lookups — a name and a company domain are sent to them in order to
  ask the question. That is the list. We do not sell personal data, and we do not
  share it for anyone else's advertising.
</p>

<h2 class="section-label" id="security">Security</h2>

<p class="quiet">
  The site is served over HTTPS. Passwords are hashed with bcrypt and API keys with
  SHA-256, so a copy of our database yields neither. Repeated failed sign-ins lock
  an account. Administrative pages are closed unless an operator token is
  configured — the failure mode of a missing setting is that nobody sees the data,
  never that everybody does.
</p>

<h2 class="section-label" id="changes">Changes</h2>

<p class="quiet">
  If this policy changes in a way that affects what we do with your data, the date
  at the top changes and the previous version is superseded from that date.
</p>

<p class="quiet">
  Questions, requests, or anything that reads wrong here:
  <a href="mailto:<%= @privacy_contact %>"><%= @privacy_contact %></a>.
</p>

<%= CsuiteFinderWeb.Layout.footer(:default) %>

</div>
</body>
</html>
